          "body": "## Problem\n\nThe opencode provider's own device-code flow polls with the server-supplied interval verbatim:\n\n```ts\n// packages/core/src/plugin/provider/opencode.ts:146\ncallback: poll(http, server, device.device_code, Duration.seconds(device.interval)),\n```\n\n`Device.interval` is an unbounded `Schema.Number` (`opencode.ts:66`). Inside `poll()` (`opencode.ts:651-673`) each iteration is `Effect.sleep(wait)` followed by a POST to `${server}/auth/device/token`, recursing on `authorization_pending`. There is **no floor and no safety margin**:\n\n- `interval: 0` or negative → `Effect.sleep` returns immediately; the loop polls as fast as HTTP round-trips allow.\n- `interval: 0.1` → 10 requests/second for the whole authorization wait.\n\nThe only bound is the integration layer's generic 10-minute attempt lifetime (`packages/core/src/integration.ts:266`), so a malformed or hostile server response buys up to ~10 minutes of maximum-rate POSTs against the token endpoint — precisely the behavior RFC 8628 §3.5 polling discipline and provider rate limits punish; the likely real-world outcome is the client getting throttled so the legitimate login then fails with `slow_down`/429s.\n\nSecondary: `authorize` never passes the device code's own `expires_in` as the authorization `expiresAt` (the integration layer supports it — `integration.ts:621`, `authorization.expiresAt ?? created + attemptLifetime`), and `poll()` never reads `device.expires_in` — the decoded field is unused, so a short-lived device code is polled for the full generic 10 minutes instead of its own lifetime.\n\n## The in-repo precedent\n\nThe OpenAI device flow got exactly this hardening in #771 — `packages/core/src/plugin/provider/openai.ts:179-188`:\n\n```ts\nexport const deviceIntervalMs = (raw: string): number => {\n  const text = raw.trim()\n  const parsed = /^\\d+$/.test(text) ? Number.parseInt(text, 10) : Number.NaN\n  return Math.max(parsed || 5, 1) * 1000\n}\n```\n\n(clamped to at least 1 second, 5-second fallback), and its poll loop sleeps `interval + pollingSafetyMargin` (`openai.ts:243`). The opencode provider's own flow — the one for this fork's own Console server — never got the same treatment. #771 covered DigitalOcean and OpenAI only; dedup search for the opencode device flow found no open or closed issue.\n\n## Evidence (main 5664199a79, read-verified this run)\n\n- `packages/core/src/plugin/provider/opencode.ts:62-67` — `Device` schema: `expires_in: Schema.Number`, `interval: Schema.Number`, both unbounded.\n- `packages/core/src/plugin/provider/opencode.ts:146` — `Duration.seconds(device.interval)` passed raw into `poll`.\n- `packages/core/src/plugin/provider/opencode.ts:651-673` — `poll`/`loop`: `Effect.sleep(wait)` → POST → recurse on `authorization_pending` with the same `wait`; `slow_down` adds 5s, but nothing ever raises a too-small initial `wait`.\n- `packages/core/src/integration.ts:266` — `attemptLifetime = Duration.minutes(10)` is the only outer bound; `:621` shows `expiresAt` is supported but unset by this provider.\n\n## Why it matters\n\nThis is the sign-in path for the fork's own provider. A Console server bug (or any endpoint that answers the device request with a bad interval) turns every login attempt into a request flood against that same server, degrading it for everyone and likely getting the user throttled out of the login they were trying to complete.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] The poll interval is floored (≥1s, mirroring `deviceIntervalMs`) with a sane fallback for non-positive/non-finite values, and a small safety margin is added like the OpenAI flow, or an explicit reason is recorded for differing.\n- [ ] `authorize` passes the device code's `expires_in` as the authorization `expiresAt` (when positive), so polling stops at the code's own lifetime instead of the generic 10 minutes.\n- [ ] A normal server interval (e.g. 5s) behaves exactly as today; `slow_down` backoff still applies on top.\n- [ ] Regression tests cover interval 0, negative, and sub-second values for the poll setup; CI green.\n",
